  "rationale": "The structural check finds no gap in either obligation.\n\n**`SourceAnalysis::parse` method.**\n- The requirement asks for `pub async fn parse(&self, id: &ResourceId) -> Result<ParsedSource, P::Error>` on `impl<P: SourceProvider> SourceAnalysis<P>`.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/src/sources.rs#impl[SourceAnalysis<P>]/fn[parse]` has that exact signature. It sits in `impl<P: SourceProvider> SourceAnalysis<P>`, which is the `impl[SourceAnalysis<P>]` item.\n- The method delegates to `self.provider.parse(id, &self.languages).await`, so it goes through the `SourceProvider` interface.\n\n**`outline_source` command on `SourceTools`.**\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/src/agent/sources.rs#impl[SourceSession<'_, P, F, D::NodeId, D::EdgeLabel>/ToolSession<D>]/fn[definitions]` declares an `outline_source` tool. Its parameters are an object with a required `resource`.\n- The same impl's `fn[dispatch]` handles `\"outline_source\" | \"read_source\"`. It deserializes a `ResourceId` from `arguments[\"resource\"]`, parses the source, and for `outline_source` returns the outline nodes' selector and kind.\n- I took the session type `SourceSession` to be the supplied `SourceTools`. The requirement text doesn't name it, so this is an inference. I did not read `dispatch` in full, so I did not trace how it reaches `SourceAnalysis::parse`.\n\nI made no behavioral or test-adequacy judgments. This was a structural assessment only.",
